#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below, continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/5a83932fdde3fa50945ddea33311a58d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0$, it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x)$, plotted below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/d83d24b9d8babe8e669ffa5fe0213550.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0$, $x=1$, and $x=2.5$. It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/fd121626f4469d5b13b86e80782627ee.png)\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2$.\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n$lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)|= 2,; lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)|= 1$.\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function breaks at $x=2$. It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99974464-05f0-5484-b5c8-5071961c2a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067248} {:answer/id #uuid "a2704f5a-4561-5560-84de-cc643e4f8fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067249} {:answer/id #uuid "e54d547a-d72f-5400-b663-6dabc5245dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067250} {:answer/id #uuid "31f05587-9d13-5056-b680-2fa23737c8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/q-48039-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067251} {:answer/id #uuid "3a19e16e-f1b4-540a-9711-0b044898f17a" :answer/type